## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a hardware-style popcount without loops or built-in functions, demonstrating understanding of bitwise manipulation techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting using bitmasks
- 32-bit unsigned arithmetic with logical shifts
- Elimination of branches and loops for performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countBits32(n) {
  n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  n = n + (n >>> 8);
  n = n + (n >>> 16);
  return n & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this differ for a 64-bit implementation?
- Can you optimize this for specific input patterns?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of branchless, scalable bit-twiddling techniques for 64-bit inputs.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR style bit reversal
- Masking and shifting sequences
- Branchless algorithms for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t n) {
  n = ((n >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1);
  n = ((n >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2);
  n = ((n >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4);
  n = ((n >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8);
  n = ((n >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16);
  n = (n >> 32) | (n << 32);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for smaller word sizes or specialized ISAs?
- How would you adapt for 128-bit inputs and performance considerations?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary 128-bit rotations without a native 128-bit type. Exercises correct handling of edge cases like r=0, r=64, and r=127, plus understanding how two 64-bit halves interact during rotation.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo halves
- Boundary-safe shifts across 64-bit parts
- Distinguishing r<64 vs r>=64 paths and their swap behavior

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned r) {
  r &= 127;
  if (r == 0) return x;
  if (r < 64) {
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << r) | (x.lo >> (64 - r));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << r) | (x.hi >> (64 - r));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned t = r - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << t) | (x.hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo >> (64 - t)) | (x.hi << t);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement rotateRight128? 
- How do you unit-test edge cases (0, 64, 127) efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform parallel, lane-specific bit manipulation on a 128-bit value without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Video: decompose 128-bit into four 32-bit words; W0 is lo, W3 is hi
- Per-lane rotation: Ri = (Wi << mi) | (Wi >> ((32 - mi) & 31)); mi = ni & 31
- Reassemble lanes in the same order; handle edge cases with safe shifts

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 rotateWords128(uint128 x, unsigned n0, unsigned n1, unsigned n2, unsigned n3) {
  unsigned m0 = n0 & 31, m1 = n1 & 31, m2 = n2 & 31, m3 = n3 & 31;
  uint32_t w0 = (uint32_t)(x.lo);
  uint32_t w1 = (uint32_t)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32_t w2 = (uint32_t)(x.hi);
  uint32_t w3 = (uint32_t)(x.hi >> 32);
  uint32_t r0 = (w0 << m0) | (w0 >> ((32 - m0) & 31));
  uint32_t r1 = (w1 << m1) | (w1 >> ((32 - m1) & 31));
  uint32_t r2 = (w2 << m2) | (w2 >> ((32 - m2) & 31));
  uint32_t r3 = (w3 << m3) | (w3 >> ((32 - m3) & 31));
  uint128 z;
  z.lo = ((uint64_t)r1 << 32) | r0;
  z.hi = ((uint64_t)r3 << 32) | r2;
  return z;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit lanes in a 256-bit value? 
- What are performance trade-offs vs. a more generic byte-wise permute?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked
This asks for deep bit-manipulation skill: a palindrome across 128 bits is a nontrivial symmetry that can be checked in constant time with a reversal network, not with loops. It reveals understanding of endian-ness and 128-bit layout.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit word as {uint64 hi, lo}
- Bit-reversal networks using masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F..., etc.
- Palindrome property across all 128 bits
- Edge-case tests (all zeros, ends-set pattern)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.hi); r.hi = reverse64(x.lo); return r;
}
int isPalindromicBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r = reverse128(x);
  return (x.hi == r.hi) && (x.lo == r.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support streaming 128-bit palindromicity with partial data?
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of 128-bit bitboards, branchless kth-one, and cross-half boundary without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; popcount and select in constant time
- Branchless kth-one within a 64-bit word
- Correct handling when n is out of range across halves

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
int findNthOne128(uint128 a, unsigned n){
  unsigned loCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.lo);
  unsigned hiCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.hi);
  unsigned total = loCnt + hiCnt;
  if (n >= total) return -1;
  if (n < loCnt) return kth1_64(a.lo, n);
  return 64 + kth1_64(a.hi, n - loCnt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 256-bit values without branches?
- What are trade-offs across compilers for portability vs performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests precise bit trick mastery and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Bit hacks
- popcount and next permutation
- Undefined behavior on max value

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextBitPerm64(x) {
  const smallest = x & -x;
  const ripple = x + smallest;
  const ones = x ^ ripple;
  const shifted = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
  return ripple | shifted;
}
```

